WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.385
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.018 --> 00:00:06.310
KEITH DANEKIND: Welcome to
the Apigee Up Close webcast.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:07.393
We do these twice a month.

00:00:07.393 --> 00:00:09.220
The topics are based
on popular demand.

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:10.880
You can sign up multiple times.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.802
And you can ask questions live.

00:00:12.802 --> 00:00:13.760
Hi, I'm Keith Danekind.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.427
And I'm a customer
engineer with Apigee,

00:00:15.427 --> 00:00:16.683
part of Google Cloud platform.

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:18.100
Today we're going
to be the top 10

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:22.103
tips to help you have a better
API program in the new year.

00:00:22.103 --> 00:00:24.520
So if you're wanting to put
together a checklist of things

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.800
you should do to make sure
you have a successful API

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.830
program in 2019, what
will be on your list?

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:31.330
I've been talking
with my colleagues

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:32.920
at Apigee about
their suggestions.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.570
And I put together a list
of the top 10 suggestions.

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:37.630
They fell roughly under
three categories--

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:42.310
process tips, product tips, and
infrastructure and DevOps tips.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:43.470
So let's get started.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.190
I'm going to start
with four topics

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:47.140
on improving your process.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:50.200
So number one, think outside in.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.180
If you've attended an
Apigee webcast before,

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:53.320
you've probably seen this.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.710
It's core to how we think about
how API management should work.

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:59.860
It's worth taking another look
in the context of this webcast.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.570
We've seen a repeated pattern
of success with our customers,

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:05.800
and it's driven by how they
think about the API problem.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.390
It requires a shift in thinking.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:09.040
Traditionally, we
think about services

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.900
from an exposure point of view.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.600
We can just expose the
service and be finished.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.090
But thinking about it from
the end user's point of view

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:17.770
leads to better results.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:20.110
Our successful customers
start with the user,

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.570
thinking about what that
user's experience will be like

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:24.820
and working
backwards from there.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.220
Providing application
developers with great APIs

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:30.130
reduces the complexity of
creating new applications.

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.350
The developer's free to
focus on user experience

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.720
and iterate on a few
different designs.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.030
The API team makes this
possible by providing

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.460
an easy to use API with
consistent interfaces

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:40.720
and security.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:42.850
The API team builds
the API facades

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.010
on the existing services,
abstracting away

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:46.420
the complexity.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.940
This makes it easy to
build great APIs that

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:50.590
are easy to use.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:53.560
So keep this pattern in mind
when you build your APIs.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:57.100
Tip number two, develop
an API product mindset.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:59.680
So APIs are not
one-off products,

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.600
but products that need
continuous iteration

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:02.420
and development.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:04.160
So focus on that lifecycle.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:06.342
So what does it mean
to productize an API?

00:02:06.342 --> 00:02:08.050
There's many aspects
we might talk about,

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.360
but we find it helpful to
focus on these three items--

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.790
outside in, speed to market,
and iterative delivery.

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:15.290
It might help to
look at an example.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:17.983
Let's look at how Walgreens
talks about their API products.

00:02:17.983 --> 00:02:19.900
They've got a bunch of
different API products.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.108
And they're split into all
these different categories

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:23.650
that you see on the slide here.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:26.080
This is very deliberate in
terms of how they package these

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:27.800
and how they manage them
and things like that.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:29.500
So Walgreens says that
their API products

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:30.917
are managed like
any other product

00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:33.400
within their digital
product portfolio.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.600
There are a few keys to success
for what that looks like.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.287
They have a product
roadmap for APIs

00:02:39.287 --> 00:02:41.620
and present it quarterly to
their entire digital product

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:43.630
team-- partners, business
teams, marketing,

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:45.260
and other key stakeholders.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.710
So there is great visibility
into what they're doing.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:49.990
And it's a great source of
opportunity and partnership

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:51.060
with other teams.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.590
Second thing, they have a
fantastic developer evangelist.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.537
And he actually sits
within the product team.

00:02:55.537 --> 00:02:57.370
So the partnership with
the product managers

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:58.073
is very strong.

00:02:58.073 --> 00:03:00.490
Next, they have great tools
to engage with their developer

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:02.957
community and manage
their API program.

00:03:02.957 --> 00:03:05.290
They have a developer portal,
which is the initial entry

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:06.527
point to their APIs.

00:03:06.527 --> 00:03:08.110
And of course, they
have Apigee, which

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:10.277
allows them to be a great
partner to their developer

00:03:10.277 --> 00:03:13.015
community.

00:03:13.015 --> 00:03:14.640
And last but definitely
not least, they

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.160
put a big focus, as
with any other product,

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.182
on defining what
success looks like

00:03:19.182 --> 00:03:20.640
and reporting out
their performance

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.930
against those metrics.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:24.650
So another tip is to create
an API product manager role.

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:26.400
That's somebody who's
going to manage APIs

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:27.893
across the entire lifecycle.

00:03:27.893 --> 00:03:30.310
They're going to take that
vision and turn it into action.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.200
And they're a subject
matter expert.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.430
So tip number three, focus
on the application developer.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:38.550
So why are API
developers so important?

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:42.810
API consumer adoption is key
to success for any API program.

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:45.780
APIs will need to be
marketed to API consumers

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:48.420
as products, not
just as raw services.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:51.600
Application developers are
your ultimate consumers,

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.300
so keep them in mind when you're
developing your API program.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.550
You may have different types
of application developers

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.800
who have different
requirements for your APIs.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.230
For example, you might
have internal application

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:03.210
developers, partners
application developers,

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.430
and external application
developers all building

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:06.960
different things,
all with different

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:09.870
wants and needs when it
comes to your API program.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.183
Shutterstock is creating a
great developer experience

00:04:12.183 --> 00:04:14.100
by answering the most
important questions that

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.529
arise during
development and creating

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:19.209
a great "getting started"
and onboarding experience.

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:22.440
So imagine that you're
an API consumer.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.080
You're an application developer,
and you go to their website.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:25.887
This is great.

00:04:25.887 --> 00:04:27.720
It's got everything you
need to get started,

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.345
and they reduce the
amount of friction,

00:04:29.345 --> 00:04:31.140
encouraging people
to develop things.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.990
So tip number
four, pick an MVBO.

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:36.270
Where do you start
with your API project?

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:39.820
Using an MVBO is a
great place to start.

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:41.310
So what is an MBVO?

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:44.400
An MVBO is a Minimum
Viable Business Outcome.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.650
It's a small yet
business-critical delivery

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:48.960
that showcases the opportunity
and starts building

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:52.450
ongoing delivery momentum.

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:54.520
So how do you pick a good MVBO?

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:56.950
The MVBO should focus
on creating milestones

00:04:56.950 --> 00:04:59.860
for business enablement and
pave the path for others

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:00.730
to follow it.

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:03.970
And the MVBO should be
ready, it should be engaging,

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.280
and it should be
sufficiently difficult.

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:08.840
Let's look at each of
those in a bit more detail.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:11.680
So ready means that
the project you pick

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.150
can get into production
quickly and demonstrate value.

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:16.250
Speed is going to
be essential here.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.730
So that means you should avoid
complex program or delivery

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:21.370
dependencies, avoid target
backend systems that

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:24.430
require significant refactoring,
avoid use cases where

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:29.390
the user base or customer
demand does not already exist.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:34.660
It should be engaging, so make
sure your project is engaging.

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:37.270
You need to deliver something
of clear business value

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:41.170
and impact aligned to strategic
goals and sponsor interest.

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:44.117
It should deliver strategic
value, deliver business value.

00:05:44.117 --> 00:05:45.700
It should resonate
with your audience,

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:47.950
and it should create a pathway
for repeatable success.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:53.100
Your MVBO should be
sufficiently difficult.

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:55.440
The APIs delivered should
not just be pass-throughs.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:58.500
They should be complex enough to
demonstrate key API management

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:02.370
benefits, including controlled
access to resources and assets,

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:05.580
analytics, self-service
consumption via a developer

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.615
portal, and they should
demonstrate how they'll scale.

00:06:08.615 --> 00:06:09.990
So consider all
those things when

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:12.030
you're thinking about an MVBO.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:16.950
Next, let's look at three
tips in the product category.

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:19.890
Number five, standardize
the security.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:22.230
Backend services often
have different protocols

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:25.950
for authentication depending
on when they were developed.

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:28.320
Navigating those multiple
security protocols

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.790
causes extra work
for API consumers.

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:32.640
Consider standardizing
the security you

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.140
present to your API consumers.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:36.432
They'll be much happier and
they'll thank you for this.

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:41.210
An API platform can help you
standardize your security.

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:43.880
In addition to policies
for authentication,

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:46.010
there's policies for
rate limiting and quotas,

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:47.570
payload protection, and more.

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:50.150
Your API platform
can control access

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:53.570
to protected resources to
help you with compliance also.

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:55.910
You may need to tie into an
existing identity management

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:56.410
system.

00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:58.250
This is an example
of a flow that

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:02.270
generates an OAuth token after
users enter their passwords.

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:05.240
The authentication flow makes a
call out to the identity system

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:07.430
to verify the user's
username and password.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:09.080
And if the credentials
are accepted,

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:11.570
it returns a token to the
application that is then

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:12.980
sent to the business flow.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:14.900
So simple example
of how you might

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:17.930
integrate with an existing
identity management system.

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:22.040
Number six, measure,
monitor, and improve.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:24.090
So how do you improve
your API program?

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:25.640
Measuring how
applications call it

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:27.098
and monitoring the
performance will

00:07:27.098 --> 00:07:29.150
help you know what
needs to be tuned

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:31.673
and give you data on how
your program is doing.

00:07:31.673 --> 00:07:34.340
So after you've built a few APIs
and your application developers

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:36.760
have discovered them and
started to send you traffic,

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:39.800
it'd great if you could
measure your API program.

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:41.570
The analytics system
in [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:44.060
allows you to measure every
aspect of your API traffic.

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:45.830
You can measure
operational details

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:48.723
like response time, frequency
of different types of errors.

00:07:48.723 --> 00:07:51.140
You can find things that are
interesting to your business,

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:53.540
like where are people located
when they call your APIs,

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:55.878
or which applications are
sending the most traffic?

00:07:55.878 --> 00:07:57.920
Maybe it's interesting to
see what type of client

00:07:57.920 --> 00:07:58.550
they're using.

00:07:58.550 --> 00:08:00.200
Are they on Internet Explorer?

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:01.250
Are they on Chrome?

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:02.942
Are they on different
mobile platforms?

00:08:02.942 --> 00:08:04.400
All of these things
can be answered

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:07.370
with analytics reports.

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:10.048
So what should you be measuring?

00:08:10.048 --> 00:08:11.840
Here's a list of some
of the common metrics

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:15.080
that we've heard from customers
that they find valuable.

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:17.720
So think about the number of
APIs, the number of developers,

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:19.880
the number of customers,
the number of partners,

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:23.045
the number of apps, the speed
to API, speed to onboard--

00:08:23.045 --> 00:08:25.670
so in other words, how long does
it take to get up and going?--

00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:29.390
traffic growth, business breath,
cost reduction, direct revenue,

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:31.257
and net promoter score.

00:08:31.257 --> 00:08:33.799
Apigee has some great reports
to help you understand your API

00:08:33.799 --> 00:08:34.299
program.

00:08:34.299 --> 00:08:36.260
For example, here is
an engagement report

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:37.850
that shows you how
many developers

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:40.919
are using your platform and
their level of engagement.

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:44.360
So one final important aspect
of operating an API Platform

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:47.240
is ensuring the
availability of your APIs.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:50.300
Apigee launched API monitoring
product a couple of weeks ago,

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:53.630
freely available now to all
cloud-based enterprise edge

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:54.270
customers.

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:55.460
So please check that out.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:59.060
So number seven, get the
right value from your APIs.

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:01.580
The value generated by
APIs does not typically

00:09:01.580 --> 00:09:04.672
come from charging
for API calls.

00:09:04.672 --> 00:09:07.130
Most of the value comes from
business opportunities enabled

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:10.070
by the APIs and the
applications that use them.

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:11.690
Make sure your
APIs deliver value,

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:13.490
either directly or indirectly.

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:17.240
Consider measuring your rate of
innovation or your time to app.

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:19.610
While an API may not
directly generate revenue,

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:22.880
what value does it bring if
you can create applications

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:24.770
in a month instead of a year?

00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:27.530
Can APIs provide you with
new sources of value?

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:30.410
Do you have unique data that
might be valuable to others?

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:32.000
Can you charge for it?

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:35.780
What new channels of
business can you explore?

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:38.330
Accuweather is able to monetize
their data by packaging it

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:41.810
into different offerings
with different price points.

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:44.290
So think of approaches for
how you might want to do this.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:49.480
Next, let's shift to
infrastructure and DevOps.

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:52.960
I have four tips
in this category.

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:55.930
Tip number eight,
automate and integrate.

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:58.390
The UI is great, but
automation is even better.

00:09:58.390 --> 00:10:00.250
Most users start
off with the UI,

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:03.170
but they'll move to automating
parts of their processes.

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:05.320
Apigee has a great
set of management APIs

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:07.840
that allow you to automate
every aspect of the platform.

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:10.420
Take a look at the reference
docs and start exploring these.

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:12.160
I've got the URL
on the screen here.

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:13.750
The Apigee community
is a great place

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:16.560
to find advice and resources
for SICD automation.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:19.060
There's a lot of people who've
gone through this before you.

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:22.450
And there's links to tools and
discussion of how to use them.

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:25.510
Number nine, grow
with microservices.

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:28.150
Microservices enable developers
to build and deploy software

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:30.640
with speed, agility, and
scale necessary to meet

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:33.970
the needs of your business.

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:36.040
You can decompose a
monolithic application

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:38.980
into a set of services that
can be developed and released

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:41.770
independently.

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:43.600
Microservices are an
architecture approach

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.220
to software development
where software

00:10:45.220 --> 00:10:48.100
is composed of small independent
services that communicate over

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:50.230
well-defined APIs.

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:52.180
Microservices enable
modern enterprises

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:53.680
to create a flexible
architecture,

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:56.200
where services can be
versioned, deployed,

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:59.777
and scaled without impact to the
other systems or applications.

00:10:59.777 --> 00:11:01.360
So the great thing
about microservices

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:03.770
is that they can easily
grow with your business.

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:06.190
But that comes with
some new complexities.

00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:09.582
An app development team may
rush to implement microservices.

00:11:09.582 --> 00:11:11.290
And when they do this,
they inadvertently

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:13.150
create silos with
inconsistencies

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:16.300
in security, visibility,
documentation, and governance.

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:18.490
Many of the APIs that
connect microservices

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:21.440
are not secured consistently
across the organization.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:23.950
So these microservices
and associate APIs

00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:26.020
are often difficult
to reuse, analyze,

00:11:26.020 --> 00:11:28.390
or even to discover
for use by their teams.

00:11:28.390 --> 00:11:30.280
You can solve this
problem by managing

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:32.020
the consumption of
your microservices

00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:33.490
with your API platform.

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:37.300
So number 10, let's talk
about cloud migration.

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:39.640
Migrating to the cloud
isn't a straight path.

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:41.260
Enterprises adopt
different paths

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:43.570
to define their cloud strategy.

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:45.490
Often, enterprises will
pick multiple paths

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:48.190
based on different
projects or workloads.

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:51.040
So there's three key
takeaways from the slide.

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:53.860
Migration paths will vary,
even within a company

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:56.050
and across applications.

00:11:56.050 --> 00:11:57.850
Migrations are not instant.

00:11:57.850 --> 00:11:59.770
Some take months or even years.

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:01.540
Across all the
paths of migration,

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:03.700
API management can
play a key role

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:05.940
in making integrations easier.

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:08.410
So how can API management help?

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:10.330
So few different
things to look at here.

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:12.820
Apigee can help with many of
these different challenges.

00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:14.170
Service connectivity.

00:12:14.170 --> 00:12:16.300
One of the primary
concerns of app migration

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:19.060
is tight coupling of the
clients or user experiences

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:21.370
to the backend services or apps.

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:24.130
Apigee acts as an abstraction
layer between the exposure

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:26.680
and consumption, allowing
for service connectivity

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:28.780
while parts of the app's
backend or front end

00:12:28.780 --> 00:12:30.910
are migrated to the cloud.

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:31.960
Security enforcement.

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:34.210
Apigee can act both as
a security enforcement

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:36.940
layer as well as a
security mediation layer.

00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:39.340
As apps are modernized
while moving to the cloud,

00:12:39.340 --> 00:12:41.290
it's very common
for the modern apps

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:44.440
to have a different security
mechanism, like JWT or OAth,

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:46.510
versus the legacy
traditional backends,

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:48.790
like SAML, BasicAuth, et cetera.

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:51.970
Apigee can bring in uniformity
by allowing the modern apps

00:12:51.970 --> 00:12:54.400
to use one common
mechanism while mediating

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:57.010
the credentials while talking
to the legacy backends, and vice

00:12:57.010 --> 00:12:58.240
versa.

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:01.390
Apigee can also make sure that
security mechanisms like regex

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:05.650
checks, DLP enforcement are
done while on-prem apps that

00:13:05.650 --> 00:13:11.440
have strict compliance needs
are calling the cloud workloads.

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:12.640
For visibility.

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:16.330
By acting as the facade
across on-prem GCP workloads,

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:18.910
Apigee can provide visibility
into how app migrations are

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:22.510
happening and the success or
traction for the migrated apps.

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:24.250
The API metrics
that Apigee provides

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:25.823
are slightly higher
level, as Apigee

00:13:25.823 --> 00:13:28.240
will have visibility into which
apps developer clients are

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:31.120
calling your API, which
API product is being used

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:33.250
to call the API, and et cetera.

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:36.340
The API metrics can be pulled in
through Apigee's management API

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:39.400
and analyzed along with other
operational metrics collected

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:43.270
by the apps logging
framework, et cetera.

00:13:43.270 --> 00:13:44.380
API facades.

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:46.870
So most often, not
all of the apps

00:13:46.870 --> 00:13:50.230
can be migrated once the
re-platform to modernized apps

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:52.660
will have to talk to
a monolith or a legacy

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:54.340
or traditional backend.

00:13:54.340 --> 00:13:56.890
These backends most of the
time will be legacy protocols

00:13:56.890 --> 00:13:58.300
like SOAP, XML.

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:01.995
It's not ideal for modern
apps to consume them that way.

00:14:01.995 --> 00:14:03.370
And it adds the
additional burden

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:04.960
of maintaining
additional libraries,

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:06.940
making the app
heavier and complex.

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:09.610
Apigee can help
create API facades

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:13.330
that are modern while
keeping the monolith intact.

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:17.020
The re-platformed or modern
apps can talk to these facades

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:20.140
instead of going
directly to the backend.

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:22.450
And finally, service management.

00:14:22.450 --> 00:14:24.730
Lift and modernization is
one of the standard themes

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:25.720
of app migration.

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:28.030
Microservices,
offered on Kubernetes,

00:14:28.030 --> 00:14:29.980
is a vehicle for
app modernization.

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:32.110
As enterprises
realize the success

00:14:32.110 --> 00:14:34.510
of microservices-based
architectures,

00:14:34.510 --> 00:14:36.430
the number of
microservices explodes,

00:14:36.430 --> 00:14:38.290
and the apps are
migrated to cloud

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:40.450
without the right
security and visibility.

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:42.160
It's extremely
difficult to accelerate

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:44.230
the adoption of microservices.

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:47.260
Some enterprises are
already looking at Istio

00:14:47.260 --> 00:14:48.220
to solve this problem.

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:50.110
For customers who
have adopted Istio,

00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:53.580
Apigee can provide enterprise
support for non-GCP Istio

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:56.850
implementations as well as add
the higher level API management

00:14:56.850 --> 00:14:59.880
aspects like OAuth
through the mixer adapter.

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:01.680
For customers who are
not looking at Istio,

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:04.500
example, customers
who are on PCF--

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:07.380
Apigee's micro gateway can
add similar capabilities

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:10.645
and manage their microservices,
like security and analytics.

00:15:15.870 --> 00:15:17.220
So that's the top 10 list.

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:19.313
Next up, let's take
some questions.

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:23.420
OK.

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:25.460
Let's start off with
the first question.

00:15:25.460 --> 00:15:28.370
Where should I start
with the management APIs?

00:15:28.370 --> 00:15:30.920
How can they be used to
automate my deployment?

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:34.910
So take a look at the
reference section of the API

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:37.070
documentation for Apigee.

00:15:37.070 --> 00:15:39.440
And in there you'll see
a list of all those.

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:41.510
Pick one or two and play
with it and just get

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:42.930
a feel for how it works.

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:44.570
One thing people
will commonly do

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:48.290
is automate the deployment
process, meaning,

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:50.960
if you're going to push
something, for example from dev

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:54.680
to test or from, maybe,
test or QA into production,

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:55.830
you can automate that.

00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:59.510
So take a look at
the management API,

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:03.380
specifically around that
area and give that a try.

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:06.410
Next question has to deal
with analytics and measuring

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:07.190
your APIs.

00:16:07.190 --> 00:16:09.560
And the question in
there is, how can I

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:11.960
measure something business
specific with a custom report

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:13.580
in Apigee?

00:16:13.580 --> 00:16:16.580
So there's a statistics
collector policy in there.

00:16:16.580 --> 00:16:18.350
The statistics
collector policy lets

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:20.720
you grab anything that's
a variable in Apigee,

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:22.670
so anything from the
request the response

00:16:22.670 --> 00:16:25.260
or anything you want to
extract out of there.

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:27.140
You can save that
as a custom metric.

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:29.430
And that can be used in
the reporting system.

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:32.090
So you can combine that
with the built in analytics,

00:16:32.090 --> 00:16:33.650
build a custom
report, and measure

00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:34.858
something that's interesting.

00:16:34.858 --> 00:16:36.320
So think about what you might--

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:38.420
what you might want
to measure with that.

00:16:38.420 --> 00:16:39.530
Next question.

00:16:39.530 --> 00:16:41.660
You touched on experience API.

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:44.120
What is an experience API?

00:16:44.120 --> 00:16:47.420
So think of an experience
as an application,

00:16:47.420 --> 00:16:52.200
or how somebody would
experience your services.

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:55.250
So a lot of times we'll
build custom services just

00:16:55.250 --> 00:16:57.140
for a particular application.

00:16:57.140 --> 00:16:59.900
You might have an
experience API to support

00:16:59.900 --> 00:17:01.460
a mobile application.

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:04.010
And what's nice about having
an API platform for this

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:05.990
is it lets you
easily build a custom

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:07.672
API that's specific
to the experience

00:17:07.672 --> 00:17:08.839
that you're trying to share.

00:17:11.410 --> 00:17:13.390
So next one in
here is a question

00:17:13.390 --> 00:17:15.579
about getting started
with using APIs

00:17:15.579 --> 00:17:17.440
for consuming microservices.

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:18.849
So what are the best practices?

00:17:18.849 --> 00:17:20.839
And how do you get
started with this?

00:17:20.839 --> 00:17:24.660
So I'm going to go back to that
outside-in type of thinking.

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:28.119
So think about how we want to
control how people experience

00:17:28.119 --> 00:17:30.610
these microservices.

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:32.560
A great place to put
Apigee is right on top

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:34.090
of those microservices.

00:17:34.090 --> 00:17:39.100
And it would be used to control
access for external consumers.

00:17:39.100 --> 00:17:41.627
So put Apigee in there
and treat the backend

00:17:41.627 --> 00:17:43.210
like you would any
other backend, only

00:17:43.210 --> 00:17:45.500
it's going to be
microservices in this case.

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:49.450
So what about measuring the
number of active subscriptions?

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:50.290
That's a great idea.

00:17:50.290 --> 00:17:53.240
So that is a good
metric to measure.

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:55.780
So you could do that through
some of the built-in reports

00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:57.700
that are in Apigee.

00:17:57.700 --> 00:17:59.830
Particularly, there's
a developer engagement

00:17:59.830 --> 00:18:01.480
one that will show
you who's subscribed

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:05.150
and what they're doing
and things like that.

00:18:05.150 --> 00:18:08.930
There's a question in here
where the listener missed

00:18:08.930 --> 00:18:10.107
some bits of authentication.

00:18:10.107 --> 00:18:12.440
What's the best way to integrate
with vendors like Octa,

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:16.170
SSO for OAuth, or
Abstract from end users?

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:17.960
So there's a great
webcast out there

00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:19.520
that you may have
seen last year.

00:18:19.520 --> 00:18:22.485
Take a look out on the
YouTube channel for Apigee.

00:18:22.485 --> 00:18:24.110
I've got another
webcast out there that

00:18:24.110 --> 00:18:25.740
just deals with that topic.

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:29.450
So basically, there are
policies inside of Apigee

00:18:29.450 --> 00:18:31.820
that will generate OAuth tokens.

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:35.210
And what you'll do is, the
generation of that token--

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:37.070
if you just put
that in your flow,

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:38.720
it's just going to
generate a token.

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:40.100
Typically, what
people will do is

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:41.767
they're going to put
some logic in front

00:18:41.767 --> 00:18:44.780
of that to generate-- or
not to generate, to call out

00:18:44.780 --> 00:18:46.190
to the identity
management system

00:18:46.190 --> 00:18:47.840
and verify the user in some way.

00:18:47.840 --> 00:18:50.200
And then if and only
if they're good will

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:51.200
they generate the token.

00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:52.730
So that's the general
pattern in there.

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:54.230
But there's a whole
webcast on that.

00:18:54.230 --> 00:18:55.730
Take a look on the
YouTube channel.

00:18:55.730 --> 00:19:00.260
And you'll see under the
Apigee Up Close series,

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:02.200
there is a webcast just
covering that topic.

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:03.410
There's a question in here.

00:19:03.410 --> 00:19:05.270
Transitioning to
the API world is

00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:08.358
disruptive to existing
internal dev community.

00:19:08.358 --> 00:19:10.400
What tips can you give
for elevating internal dev

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:13.970
community learning, how best
to promote and encourage?

00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:19.410
So I would explore, why is it
disruptive to move to the API

00:19:19.410 --> 00:19:19.910
world?

00:19:19.910 --> 00:19:23.060
And what is it about that?

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:24.950
Some of the things
we'd focus on is,

00:19:24.950 --> 00:19:27.410
what's the onboarding
process for APIs?

00:19:27.410 --> 00:19:30.910
So is it easier if you have
an API management platform

00:19:30.910 --> 00:19:31.410
in place?

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:33.660
Is there a developer portal
where they can go and find

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:35.520
examples and things like that.

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:38.930
So it should be something the
developers are excited about.

00:19:38.930 --> 00:19:41.472
And it should be something
that can be done in a way

00:19:41.472 --> 00:19:42.680
that it's not too disruptive.

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:46.350
So explore where the friction
is in there and work on that.

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:48.320
So what best describes
the attributes

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:50.030
of an API or a digital team?

00:19:50.030 --> 00:19:53.900
So we'll rephrase this
as, who's on that team

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:54.830
or what they're doing.

00:19:54.830 --> 00:19:56.952
So when you're first
getting started,

00:19:56.952 --> 00:19:58.160
this might be a smaller team.

00:19:58.160 --> 00:19:59.577
There might be
people on there who

00:19:59.577 --> 00:20:01.640
are going to be building APIs.

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:04.460
And those might be people who
built web services in the past,

00:20:04.460 --> 00:20:06.860
or at least they have a
knowledge of what the back end

00:20:06.860 --> 00:20:09.200
services are and
can look at those

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:11.330
and figure out what to expose.

00:20:11.330 --> 00:20:14.960
Over time we'd like to add an
API product manager because we

00:20:14.960 --> 00:20:16.910
want to think about
these things as products,

00:20:16.910 --> 00:20:18.630
manage their lifecycle,
and things like that.

00:20:18.630 --> 00:20:20.672
So that's going to be
somebody who's a little bit

00:20:20.672 --> 00:20:22.340
more business focused.

00:20:22.340 --> 00:20:23.870
You may have some
people on there

00:20:23.870 --> 00:20:26.570
who are viewing reports
and measuring things.

00:20:26.570 --> 00:20:30.890
They'd probably fall into that
product manager role, also.

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:33.980
You may have some
people in there

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:36.140
who are going to be
consuming these APIs.

00:20:36.140 --> 00:20:38.690
But the team varies quite a bit.

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:41.170
You'll find a lot of different
people on the API team.

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:42.170
There's a question here.

00:20:42.170 --> 00:20:44.030
For enterprises
with B2B partners,

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:47.120
can Apigee play an outbound
API call management

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:51.510
role, management of calls
originating from inside?

00:20:51.510 --> 00:20:52.810
So yeah, we do see this.

00:20:52.810 --> 00:20:54.530
So the pattern here
is that you can

00:20:54.530 --> 00:20:55.950
manage any kind of API call.

00:20:55.950 --> 00:20:58.400
So maybe you're calling
out or maybe you're

00:20:58.400 --> 00:21:00.290
receiving calls coming in.

00:21:00.290 --> 00:21:03.200
Either way, you can think
of this as a good place

00:21:03.200 --> 00:21:04.700
to have API
management, if you want

00:21:04.700 --> 00:21:06.658
to be able to measure
that traffic, if you want

00:21:06.658 --> 00:21:09.620
to be able to control it,
reshape requests, and things

00:21:09.620 --> 00:21:10.610
like that.

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:13.038
You can use it for inside
calls or for outside calls

00:21:13.038 --> 00:21:14.580
and whether you're
calling in or out.

00:21:14.580 --> 00:21:16.205
Question here is, is
there such a thing

00:21:16.205 --> 00:21:18.260
as a private, internal API?

00:21:18.260 --> 00:21:20.520
If yes, how is it
treated differently?

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:22.940
So private or internal API--

00:21:22.940 --> 00:21:24.830
I might think of that
as an experience API.

00:21:24.830 --> 00:21:27.440
And really, the only thing
that's going to make it private

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:29.870
versus public is how
you choose to expose it

00:21:29.870 --> 00:21:33.830
and who has access to
it and who can use it.

00:21:33.830 --> 00:21:35.180
But we see this commonly.

00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:39.350
We'll see people have
completely big internal API

00:21:39.350 --> 00:21:41.203
programs at their
companies, where they're

00:21:41.203 --> 00:21:43.370
developing all these things
and none of it's exposed

00:21:43.370 --> 00:21:44.210
externally.

00:21:44.210 --> 00:21:46.040
So that's a very
common use case.

00:21:46.040 --> 00:21:48.920
Next question-- can Apigee
support custom transformations?

00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:51.920
Also, does Apigee support
API orchestration?

00:21:51.920 --> 00:21:55.280
So for custom transformations,
yes, very much so.

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:58.160
So we have built in
transformations in there

00:21:58.160 --> 00:22:00.860
for JSON to XML, XML to JSON.

00:22:00.860 --> 00:22:04.280
You can also use XXLT in there
if you're working with XML.

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:07.490
And there's support for
using different languages

00:22:07.490 --> 00:22:08.810
in there for custom policy.

00:22:08.810 --> 00:22:11.712
So Java, JavaScript,
and Python can be used.

00:22:11.712 --> 00:22:13.670
And they have complete
control over the request

00:22:13.670 --> 00:22:14.660
and the response flow.

00:22:14.660 --> 00:22:16.180
So one thing that's
very common is

00:22:16.180 --> 00:22:17.930
you might write a
little bit of JavaScript

00:22:17.930 --> 00:22:19.160
or Java code in there.

00:22:19.160 --> 00:22:22.490
And that would transform the
response that comes back.

00:22:22.490 --> 00:22:24.200
That's a common use case.

00:22:24.200 --> 00:22:25.910
On the second side
of that, can Apigee

00:22:25.910 --> 00:22:28.040
support API orchestration?

00:22:28.040 --> 00:22:29.520
Yes, that's a common use case.

00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:31.058
So you're going to
have one call in,

00:22:31.058 --> 00:22:33.350
and then you might call two
or three different services

00:22:33.350 --> 00:22:35.840
on the backend, collect
those responses,

00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:37.040
and orchestrate them.

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:38.800
So yeah, you could do that.

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:40.550
There's direct call
outs to do that if you

00:22:40.550 --> 00:22:41.450
want to do it synchronously.

00:22:41.450 --> 00:22:43.130
If you want to do
it asynchronously,

00:22:43.130 --> 00:22:45.710
you can do it with some of the
built-in programming languages

00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:46.790
for the custom policies.

00:22:46.790 --> 00:22:48.832
The question here is a
response to the other one.

00:22:48.832 --> 00:22:50.780
Thank you for clarifying
on experience APIs.

00:22:50.780 --> 00:22:52.460
How does it work
with API facades?

00:22:52.460 --> 00:22:54.810
Do they complement or
supplement each other?

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:58.190
So an experience API
is really something

00:22:58.190 --> 00:23:02.420
that's custom built to support
a particular application.

00:23:02.420 --> 00:23:05.250
A facade I would describe
as, what you're doing

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:08.100
is you're hiding away the
complexities of the enterprise.

00:23:08.100 --> 00:23:11.820
So you're separating
the external interface

00:23:11.820 --> 00:23:14.560
from the internal
implementation of that API.

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:18.030
So think about, how
would you ideally like

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:20.070
to have this API look
to the outside world?

00:23:20.070 --> 00:23:22.870
And build an interface
that looks like that.

00:23:22.870 --> 00:23:24.990
And then map that to
the internal services.

00:23:24.990 --> 00:23:27.460
And that can be done in a
number of different ways.

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:29.460
But really, the difference
between these two

00:23:29.460 --> 00:23:30.810
is the intention.

00:23:30.810 --> 00:23:33.690
Are we building this
for a specific use?

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:35.220
Or is this just
a general purpose

00:23:35.220 --> 00:23:38.910
way to improve and simplify
how our API is to call?

00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:41.640
How do you demonstrate the
value of API-driven architecture

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:43.260
within an enterprise
if they're happy

00:23:43.260 --> 00:23:45.480
with an ESB-led architecture?

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:47.020
Yeah, that's a tough one.

00:23:47.020 --> 00:23:52.150
So I would say, once they get
to see something like this,

00:23:52.150 --> 00:23:53.820
they would absolutely love it.

00:23:53.820 --> 00:23:58.200
If I'm thinking old school
ESB-style architecture, where

00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:01.290
this would be better
is, think about,

00:24:01.290 --> 00:24:03.918
how long does it take you
to develop an application?

00:24:03.918 --> 00:24:06.210
So if you wanted to write a
[INAUDIBLE] single page web

00:24:06.210 --> 00:24:08.430
application or a new
mobile application,

00:24:08.430 --> 00:24:10.380
what's that process like,
and how long does it

00:24:10.380 --> 00:24:12.150
take to do something like that?

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:14.280
Also, where's the documentation?

00:24:14.280 --> 00:24:15.690
Is there a developer
portal today

00:24:15.690 --> 00:24:17.648
that has everything
somebody would be on there?

00:24:17.648 --> 00:24:19.925
What's the process for
a developer to onboard?

00:24:19.925 --> 00:24:21.300
So those are a
few things to look

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:22.530
at that might help with that.

00:24:22.530 --> 00:24:24.760
Can you map existing
microservices to Apigee?

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:26.050
How can you achieve that?

00:24:26.050 --> 00:24:27.450
So yeah, you sure can.

00:24:27.450 --> 00:24:30.450
So think about it like
the backend is going

00:24:30.450 --> 00:24:32.400
to be a set of microservices.

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:34.920
So you're going to build
a facade on top of that,

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:36.900
build a proxy in Apigee.

00:24:36.900 --> 00:24:39.580
And that will talk to those
various backend systems.

00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:40.080
OK.

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:41.610
A few more questions coming in.

00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:43.890
What's my opinion
on API versioning,

00:24:43.890 --> 00:24:47.700
putting the version at the
level the path or the content?

00:24:47.700 --> 00:24:50.310
So this is one of those
things that's very

00:24:50.310 --> 00:24:52.410
contentious in the API world.

00:24:52.410 --> 00:24:53.460
How do you do versioning?

00:24:53.460 --> 00:24:56.050
I've seen many different
ways to do this.

00:24:56.050 --> 00:24:58.950
The most common I have
seen, and the advice

00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:03.570
that we tend to give, is
put the version in the URL,

00:25:03.570 --> 00:25:06.600
and keep it as far to
the left as possible.

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:09.120
So that's the most
common we see.

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:10.730
I've seen it done
in headers, though.

00:25:10.730 --> 00:25:12.730
I've seen it done in a
number of different ways.

00:25:12.730 --> 00:25:14.522
But that's the most
common that I'm seeing.

00:25:17.110 --> 00:25:20.140
Does Apigee Istio replace
existing middleware integration

00:25:20.140 --> 00:25:23.080
and adapter technologies?

00:25:23.080 --> 00:25:26.170
Istio would help you manage
a set of microservices.

00:25:26.170 --> 00:25:28.840
Sometimes those microservice
might be replacing

00:25:28.840 --> 00:25:30.610
internal technologies.

00:25:30.610 --> 00:25:32.630
Sometimes they might
be supplementing them.

00:25:32.630 --> 00:25:36.010
I'm seeing a lot of folks do
microservices for new projects.

00:25:36.010 --> 00:25:39.258
And they would be complementary
or supplemental to existing

00:25:39.258 --> 00:25:40.300
things that are in place.

00:25:40.300 --> 00:25:40.780
OK.

00:25:40.780 --> 00:25:42.405
I'm going to take a
few more questions,

00:25:42.405 --> 00:25:45.147
and then we'll
move on to a whole.

00:25:45.147 --> 00:25:46.480
Thank you for all the questions.

00:25:46.480 --> 00:25:47.760
I've got a lot of them in here.

00:25:47.760 --> 00:25:48.885
There's a question in here.

00:25:48.885 --> 00:25:51.120
Is there a way to onboard
an API developer manually?

00:25:51.120 --> 00:25:53.160
Currently, the
developer subscription

00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:54.180
is done automatically.

00:25:54.180 --> 00:25:55.680
I have a use case
where I want to do

00:25:55.680 --> 00:25:58.020
some verification before
granting the subscription.

00:25:58.020 --> 00:25:59.350
Is this possible?

00:25:59.350 --> 00:26:00.450
So yes, it is.

00:26:00.450 --> 00:26:02.490
You can check in Apigee--

00:26:02.490 --> 00:26:05.380
you can set up that it's
going to be done manually.

00:26:05.380 --> 00:26:08.102
And then you can go
into the dashboard

00:26:08.102 --> 00:26:09.060
where you approve apps.

00:26:09.060 --> 00:26:10.810
And you would have to
approve it in there.

00:26:10.810 --> 00:26:12.910
It can also be done
through an API.

00:26:12.910 --> 00:26:13.410
OK.

00:26:13.410 --> 00:26:15.870
I'll take two more,
and then we'll wrap up.

00:26:15.870 --> 00:26:17.670
Can Apigee leverage
cloud functions

00:26:17.670 --> 00:26:20.640
or use a cloud function
as the backend API source?

00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:22.650
Can it use Pubsub for queuing?

00:26:22.650 --> 00:26:24.790
So yes, absolutely.

00:26:24.790 --> 00:26:28.530
So a few different
ways to do this.

00:26:28.530 --> 00:26:31.800
We've got some connectors that
are in Apigee for Google cloud

00:26:31.800 --> 00:26:32.643
platform.

00:26:32.643 --> 00:26:34.560
So if you're working on
Google cloud platform,

00:26:34.560 --> 00:26:36.660
you'll find built
in support for this.

00:26:36.660 --> 00:26:40.805
If you happen to be working
on AWS or another cloud,

00:26:40.805 --> 00:26:42.180
check our
documentation for that.

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:45.600
There are some examples
of doing lambda functions.

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:48.840
Or in other cases,
as long as something

00:26:48.840 --> 00:26:53.220
has an API that can be
connected to through HTTP,

00:26:53.220 --> 00:26:54.810
this is going to be
pretty easy to do.

00:26:54.810 --> 00:26:57.600
What's the preferred approach
to restrict access to APIs

00:26:57.600 --> 00:27:00.750
protected by Apigee so traffic
must go through Apigee first?

00:27:00.750 --> 00:27:03.700
Firewall rules,
TLS certificates--

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:04.840
how do we do that?

00:27:04.840 --> 00:27:07.920
So yes, firewall rules
and TLS certificates

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:09.340
would be two ways to do that.

00:27:09.340 --> 00:27:13.260
But basically, some form
of cutting off access that

00:27:13.260 --> 00:27:14.400
doesn't go through Apigee.

00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:17.350
And that's typically done
through networking techniques.

00:27:17.350 --> 00:27:17.850
All right.

00:27:17.850 --> 00:27:20.970
Our next webcast
coming up on January 17

00:27:20.970 --> 00:27:24.150
is hybrid apps, migration,
and modernization and the need

00:27:24.150 --> 00:27:25.840
for API management.

00:27:25.840 --> 00:27:27.090
Thank you for attending today.

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:29.040
And happy new year
to everyone out there

00:27:29.040 --> 00:27:31.130
from everyone at Apigee.

